  "writeup": "L35 N8136 is a tonic Spanish-language indicator, not a spiky feature detector: mean |activation| is 89.5 on raw Spanish and 67.1 inside few-shot translation prompts (about half of whose tokens are Spanish) versus 3.6 on English and 3.3 on math/code -- a ~25x tonic ratio, with consistently negative polarity. It fires on essentially every Spanish token (the >2-sigma share is *lower* on Spanish, 1.4%, than English, 6.2%, precisely because the Spanish level is uniformly high rather than peaked), hardest on high-frequency function words (las, los, una, mas) and Spanish morphology. Its rare English activations are on non-English-looking proper nouns (Dilokrit Patt, Aldwych, Vic). This matches the late-layer language-identity features of Harrasse et al. rather than a translation-task switch. The neighbours are graded rather than binary: 9490/11333 are elevated on all natural language with a Spanish>English tilt and spike on subword fragments (retro, ud, ño); 2972/9307/3436 sit between, suggesting a small late-layer language-identity subspace with 8136 as its strongest axis.",
  "command": "uv run python -m scripts.phase1b_outlier qwen3-8b"
}
